00:17:04,580 --> 00:17:06,898
Now we are ready to start. Listen carefully.

187
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:36,542
Boys and girls have been taught together.

188
00:17:36,552 --> 00:17:41,238
Recently, though, some schools are reconsidering separating classes.

189
00:17:41,248 --> 00:17:43,880
This is different than in the past, however.

190
00:17:43,890 --> 00:17:47,630
Girls still learn the same subjects as boys.

191
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:50,725
They just study without boys in the classroom.

192
00:17:50,735 --> 00:17:55,052
Why? Well, scientists know that boys and girls learn differently.

193
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:05,110
They can show what they know.

194
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:12,390
In general, girls are quieter. They prefer to listen and think carefully before they give their answers.

195
00:18:12,400 --> 00:18:17,414
When boys and girls are together, the boys can be too dominant.

196
00:18:17,424 --> 00:18:21,174
Also, boys and girls can bother each other.

197
00:18:21,184 --> 00:18:25,370
Instead of thinking about the lesson, they think about each other.

198
00:18:25,380 --> 00:18:29,880
In a separated classroom, they behave better and can focus on studying.

199
00:18:33,520 --> 00:18:37,310
The difference is that boys and girls often like different things.

200
00:18:37,320 --> 00:18:42,070
For instance, boys prefer stories with action and adventure.

201
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:47,748
In an all-boys English class, the teacher can choose books that will be interesting to boys.

202
00:18:47,758 --> 00:18:52,370
This way, they will be more excited about the stories.

203
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:59,980
Not all the classes are separated, just the main subjects, like English, math, science, and social studies.

204
00:19:03,520 --> 00:19:06,270
Separate education is not good.

205
00:19:06,280 --> 00:19:10,934
They worry that boys and girls will not learn how to work well together.

206
00:19:10,944 --> 00:19:15,734
Right now, less than 600 schools are trying separate classrooms.

207
00:19:15,744 --> 00:19:21,790
Time will show if it is a good idea or something that should be left in the past.

208
00:19:21,800 --> 00:19:30,885
Now open your question paper and look at Talker Lecture 3. You now have 35 seconds to look through the questions and the options in each question.

209
00:19:34,320 --> 00:19:56,090
Now we are ready to start. Listen carefully.

210
00:19:56,100 --> 00:19:57,129
Hi!

211
00:19:57,139 --> 00:20:01,990
I'd like to welcome all of you to the International Student Orientation at Norwalk College.

212
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:08,150
I hope that your travels from your home country have been easy and that you are all settling in well.

213
00:20:08,160 --> 00:20:23,958
I believe I've met everyone, but just in case, I'm the Director for International Student Affairs, the person you can come to if you're having problems with your classes, your housing, visas. You're always welcome to stop by my office.

214
00:20:23,968 --> 00:20:29,880
We'll have several short workshops today, and we'll be talking about some common issues that international students face.

215
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:38,390
The first one that I want to deal with is about communication, and in particular, nonverbal communication.

216
00:20:38,400 --> 00:20:42,870
Now, all of you have been studying English for some time.

217
00:20:42,880 --> 00:20:51,035
You have a good vocabulary, and you probably know all about verb tenses and modals and can ask directions and order from a menu.

218
00:20:51,045 --> 00:20:58,966
But communication is not only verbal. That is, talking is not the only language we use to communicate.

219
00:20:58,976 --> 00:21:01,990
Another way we communicate is through nonverbal communication.

220
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,530
Or body language.

221
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:20,054
The term body language means the movements we use and the facial expressions we have, like smiling or frowning, the way we sit or stand, the way we touch or look at someone, the distance we stand from another person.

222
00:21:20,064 --> 00:21:26,670
Some psychologists say that more than 60% of our communication is actually done through body language.

223
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:34,422
We do it without thinking or without ever having been specifically taught what the gestures or expressions mean.

224
00:21:34,432 --> 00:21:42,825
The problem is that body language is a little bit different in each culture, and this can often cause some problems with communication.

225
00:21:42,835 --> 00:21:44,500
Here's an example.

226
00:21:44,510 --> 00:21:53,010
In many places, it may be a little rude or disrespectful to look at another person directly in their eyes as you talk with them.

227
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:57,410
So many international students will avoid this eye contact.

228
00:21:57,420 --> 00:21:59,440
But in America, we expect it.

229
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:05,416
Eye contact is a sign of honesty and straightforwardness.

230
00:22:05,426 --> 00:22:11,586
American parents actually encourage their children to look people in the eye when speaking.

231
00:22:11,596 --> 00:22:19,830
Just imagine a conversation where an international student was trying to be respectful by not looking into the eyes of her American classmate.

232
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:29,310
The American might wonder why her classmate wouldn't look directly at her, and the international classmate would be wondering why she was being stared at.

233
00:22:29,320 --> 00:22:33,455
The end result is an awkward and frustrating interaction.

234
00:22:33,465 --> 00:22:35,168
Oh, here's another one.

235
00:22:35,178 --> 00:22:44,086
Americans shake hands firmly when they greet each other, because to Americans, a firm handshake signifies strength and power.

236
00:22:44,096 --> 00:22:49,710
But a soft or limp handshake is considered a sign of weakness.

237
00:22:49,720 --> 00:22:56,330
In some cultures, handshakes are less common, or a gentle, soft handshake is acceptable.

238
00:22:56,340 --> 00:23:02,450
So students need to know that when shaking hands, it's important to be firm.

239
00:23:02,460 --> 00:23:06,742
In America, a thumbs-up sign, a thumb raised in the air...

240
00:23:06,752 --> 00:23:08,726
That is the end of Part 3.

241
00:23:08,736 --> 00:23:13,900
